From 6238a40fb06499273bbbe7422288c67432153c5f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tangelo Dev <tangelo-dev@noreply.invalid>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:41:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cecd: give OutBox messages an ID and creation time like the
 real module

Applications write StreetPass messages with an all-zero message ID and
no timestamps; the real cecd module fills them in. HLE CECD stored the
application's header as is, so every message was named _AAAAAAAAAAA.
NetPass (and the receiving cecd) drop a message whose ID is already in
the inbox, so after an exchange only the first Tangelo player's message
would ever arrive.

Compared against a message the real module wrote for Nintendo 3DS Sound
(online LLE modules on): it generated a random 8-byte message ID and
stamped the creation time; sender ID, the second message ID and the
send/receive times stay 0.

- WriteMessage / WriteMessageWithHMAC to the OutBox now do the same:
  a random ID when the message has none, the emulated clock as creation
  time when it has none, the file named after the ID, and the ID
  returned to the application. Inbox writes are unchanged.
- The timestamp struct had month, day, hour, ... but the real layout
  has a weekday after the day and a 16-bit millisecond at the end
  (2026-09-28 Monday 22:35:00.370 = 2026, 9, 28, 1, 22, 35, 0, 370).
  ZipPass wrote imported messages' send/receive times with the old
  layout, shifting every field by one byte; it now uses the right one.

